@namespace NSail.Components
@typeparam TValue
@inherits NsFieldBase<TValue>

@using NSail.Localization

@inject LanguageProvider Language

@* Both pickers name the culture for the same reason NsDateField does: left unset, the
   vendor lays out its calendar — month names, first day of the week — against the ambient
   CurrentCulture, which is the request's on the server and the BROWSER's on the WebAssembly
   client. The two disagree the moment a reader's browser is not the app's language — measured
   on this control, same page and same instant, server "4/8/2026" against client "8/5/2026" —
   and a day-first reading of a month-first string is a silently wrong date, not a formatting
   nuisance. The text half of that answer is the converter's (DateText), which is handed the
   same culture rather than reading one of its own. *@
<div class="d-flex flex-column gap-2">
    @* Converter before Date, for the reason NsDateField's own copy of this picker carries: the
       vendor writes its box from the converter as Date is assigned, and Blazor assigns in the
       order written here. *@
    <MudDatePicker Converter="_text"
                   Date="MudDate"
                   DateChanged="OnDateChanged"
                   TextChanged="OnTextChanged"
                   Label="@GetLabel()"
                   Placeholder="@GetPlaceholder()"
                   Required="IsRequired"
                   Disabled="IsDisabled"
                   ReadOnly="IsReadOnly"
                   Editable="true"
                   Culture="@RegionCulture.For(Language)"
                   Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                   Margin="Margin.Dense"
                   Error="@HasError()"
                   ErrorText="@GetErrorText()"
                   UserAttributes="InputAttributes" />
    @* The two boxes are ONE field, so a refusal marks both of them, for the eye and for the
       tree: a red date box beside a grey time box reads as the hour being fine when what was
       refused is the instant they spell together. The sentence stays the date box's — one field
       says one message however many boxes it wears — which is why the mark arrives as the
       vendor's own error class instead of its Error parameter (TimeMark). *@
    <MudTimePicker Time="MudTime"
                   TimeChanged="OnTimeChanged"
                   Disabled="IsDisabled"
                   ReadOnly="IsReadOnly"
                   Culture="@RegionCulture.For(Language)"
                   Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                   Margin="Margin.Dense"
                   Class="@TimeMark"
                   UserAttributes="@TimeAttributes" />
</div>
<NsFieldHelper Text="@Helper" Message="@GetErrorText()" DescribedBy="@DescribedBy" />
